WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:07.520
So with that, we have learned the standalone CTE and the necessity.

00:07.720 --> 00:12.160
And both of them belongs to a type called Non-recursive CTE.

00:12.400 --> 00:14.120
So what is a non-recursive CTE?

00:14.280 --> 00:17.720
It means it is a city that is executed only once.

00:17.720 --> 00:23.560
So there is no repetitions or looping or anything so that SQL can executed in one go and that's it.

00:23.560 --> 00:27.400
But in the other hand, the recursive CTE is exactly the opposite.

00:27.400 --> 00:28.640
So a recursive CTE.

00:28.680 --> 00:36.600
It is a self-referencing query that repeatedly processing the data until a certain condition is met.

00:36.600 --> 00:42.400
And we usually use the recursive CTE if we have like hierarchical structure and we want to navigate

00:42.400 --> 00:44.320
and travel through the hierarchy.

00:44.360 --> 00:46.520
I know this might be confusing, but don't worry about it.

00:46.520 --> 00:48.280
We can have very simple examples.

00:48.320 --> 00:52.000
Now again we have our tables in the database and we have CTE.

00:52.280 --> 00:55.640
Now the query of the CTE is going to be executed for the first time.

00:55.640 --> 00:59.400
And in the results we are going to have the initial data from the CTE.

00:59.400 --> 01:01.590
city, but it is not everything yet.

01:01.630 --> 01:07.590
Now this intermediate result is not ready yet for the main query, but instead of that, it's going

01:07.630 --> 01:09.070
to go back to the CTE.

01:09.390 --> 01:15.230
And the CTE is going to check whether the current results is meeting a specific condition.

01:15.270 --> 01:20.110
So now if the check says no, it's not meeting the condition, what can happen?

01:20.150 --> 01:23.830
The CTE query can be executed for the second time.

01:23.830 --> 01:26.430
So as you can see we are looping through the CTE.

01:26.590 --> 01:33.590
Now the result of the second iteration the second execution will be added to the intermediate results.

01:33.590 --> 01:36.630
So now the intermediate result has more data.

01:36.630 --> 01:41.550
And again before we can use it from the main query it's going to be checked from the CTE.

01:41.830 --> 01:44.270
Does the result fulfill the condition?

01:44.270 --> 01:47.990
If it's still no then go and execute the CTE again.

01:47.990 --> 01:50.190
So we're going to have a third iteration.

01:50.190 --> 01:53.790
And a new data are going to be added to the intermediate results.

01:53.790 --> 01:55.670
So this is our third iteration.

01:55.670 --> 01:57.630
Now it's going to be checked again from the CTE.

01:57.950 --> 01:59.900
Did we fulfill the condition?

02:00.020 --> 02:04.100
If the answer is yes, then the loop cannot break and everything ends.

02:04.100 --> 02:06.820
So there will be no fourth iteration of the CTE.

02:06.860 --> 02:09.540
So with that, the city says, okay, I'm done.

02:09.580 --> 02:13.420
This is the final result of the intermediate result.

02:13.460 --> 02:19.420
Then the loop cannot break and everything ends, and the CTE will not be executed for the first time.

02:19.460 --> 02:21.780
And now the city can say, okay, I'm done.

02:21.820 --> 02:27.140
Now my intermediate result is ready to be used from the main query, and now nothing new happens.

02:27.140 --> 02:32.220
The main query can go and retrieve the data from the intermediate results and do some magic in order

02:32.220 --> 02:34.100
to prepare the final results.

02:34.100 --> 02:38.220
So that means there will be no iterations or looping inside the main query.

02:38.220 --> 02:43.300
The loop is going to be happen only in the CTE, and that's why we call it recursive CTE.

02:43.460 --> 02:48.860
So now if you compare it with the other types, all other types are always in one direction, and all

02:48.860 --> 02:54.940
the city is going to be executed only once, but the recursive city is going to be keep looping until

02:54.940 --> 03:00.850
the condition is met, and only then it can forward the data to the main query, and normally we use

03:00.850 --> 03:02.170
the recursive ctes.

03:02.370 --> 03:05.130
If you are navigating through hierarchical structure.

03:05.130 --> 03:10.250
So if you have in your data like hierarchical structures, you can go and use the recursive cities in

03:10.250 --> 03:11.690
order to navigate through it.

03:11.730 --> 03:13.810
So this is the recursive city.

03:13.850 --> 03:14.050
Okay.

03:14.090 --> 03:16.410
So now let's check the syntax of the recursive CTE.

03:16.570 --> 03:19.370
It is a little bit complicated but we're going to do it step by step.

03:19.450 --> 03:20.330
So what do we have.

03:20.370 --> 03:23.570
We have a query and we would like to put it in a city.

03:23.610 --> 03:29.970
So we're going to have the usual stuff with clause the name of the city and us and then the query.

03:30.130 --> 03:32.370
So this is the definition of our city.

03:32.410 --> 03:35.730
But now if you leave it like this SQL can execute it only once.

03:35.730 --> 03:37.890
But we would like to make a loop iteration.

03:37.890 --> 03:44.610
So in order to do that we have to go and define a second select statement inside our CTE like this.

03:44.610 --> 03:46.450
So we are selecting the data.

03:46.650 --> 03:49.370
And here we have to define a breaking condition.

03:49.370 --> 03:53.890
So here in the second query we are defining a condition in order to break the loop.

03:54.170 --> 03:57.370
Otherwise it's going to loop for infinite or the system going to break.

03:57.410 --> 04:02.210
You could use it in the Where clause, or you can use it even in an inner join, because both of them

04:02.210 --> 04:05.490
are filtering the data, and you can use it in order to break the condition.

04:05.530 --> 04:05.890
All right.

04:05.890 --> 04:08.050
So now still there is something missing.

04:08.050 --> 04:10.370
How are we going to make like things looping.

04:10.370 --> 04:13.330
Well we have to reference this CTE to itself.

04:13.330 --> 04:18.730
So what we're going to do we're going to say the second query going to select the data from the same

04:18.730 --> 04:19.250
CTE.

04:19.450 --> 04:22.570
So that means we have now a query that is querying itself.

04:22.570 --> 04:24.130
And this is of course what we want.

04:24.170 --> 04:27.250
We want to make iterations and we want to make a loop.

04:27.250 --> 04:29.730
That's why we have to go and reference it to itself.

04:29.770 --> 04:31.970
And now in SQL you cannot have it like this.

04:31.970 --> 04:35.050
You cannot have like two Select statements in one query.

04:35.090 --> 04:36.530
You have to connect it somehow.

04:36.570 --> 04:41.730
That's why we can go and use the union all or union depend if you want to have duplicates or not.

04:41.730 --> 04:45.090
So now we call the first query the anchor query.

04:45.090 --> 04:50.530
The anchor query is going to be the first query that interacts with the database and provide us the

04:50.530 --> 04:52.770
initial intermediate results.

04:52.770 --> 04:59.000
So it is the starting point of the iteration, and we can say it is the first step in the process.

04:59.120 --> 05:04.040
So this is going to be executed only once, and it's going to provide us the initial step, the first

05:04.040 --> 05:05.120
step in the process.

05:05.120 --> 05:08.520
Now we call the second step as a recursive query.

05:08.520 --> 05:09.560
And we call it like this.

05:09.560 --> 05:16.760
Because this query can be executed multiple times and it will keep repeating and add data to the intermediate

05:16.760 --> 05:19.880
results until the condition is met.

05:19.920 --> 05:24.080
Or let's say there will be no more data that is available to be processed.

05:24.080 --> 05:27.600
So this is the syntax of the CTE query for the main query.

05:27.640 --> 05:28.720
Nothing is changed.

05:28.720 --> 05:32.840
So we have to go and use the CTE name in the main query.

05:33.080 --> 05:36.080
So this is the syntax of the recursive CTE.

05:36.200 --> 05:41.480
So think about it like this SQL going to go and execute the anchor query only once.

05:41.600 --> 05:45.080
And then after that SQL going to go through the recursive query.

05:45.080 --> 05:50.440
And keep looping and looping and iterating until a certain condition is met.

05:50.440 --> 05:53.040
And then SQL is going to go out from the CTE.

05:53.200 --> 05:56.830
So this is actually what we mean with the anchor and recursive queries.

05:56.870 --> 05:57.150
All right.

